{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus outcomes across most horizons (typically +0.2 to +1.5 early, widening to +5 to +12 by weeks 3–4). Apply a downward level shift.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: the overprediction increases with lead time (error grows roughly monotonically after ~day 10). Use a lead-time scaling correction (small adjustment days 1–5, moderate days 6–15, large days 16–30).",
    "Underestimates drawdowns / too-strong mean reversion upward: when recent history is falling, the model predicts stabilization/rebound too early and too high; true series continues trending down. Add a trend-following term that extends recent negative momentum into the forecast.",
    "Rebound overshoot: in the back half of the horizon the model often projects a recovery to mid-70s while realized prices keep sliding to low/mid-60s. Cap rebound magnitude and delay turning points (shift inflection later).",
    "Volatility compression: forecast path is too smooth and centered above realized declines; increase downside sensitivity (asymmetric error correction) so negative shocks propagate more than positive ones."
  ]
}
